    <code code="11450-4" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Problem List"/>
    <title>Problems</title>
    <text><table><thead><tr><th>Problem</th><th>Clinical status</th><th>Verification</th><th>Code</th><th>Provenance</th></tr></thead><tbody>
  <tr ID="problem-1">
    <td>Generalized tonic-clonic seizure on a flight two and a half weeks ago, in a 36-year-old woman who was observed overnight in hospital elsewhere and has had treatment for seizures in the past, records requested; no antiseizure medication now; normal examination today. Evaluated as a new seizure: EEG and MRI of the brain with susceptibility-weighted imaging ordered, neurology (epilepsy) review booked, and no driving</td><td>resolved</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>R56.9</td><td>S — Synthetic presenting problem anchored to her publicly reported seizure on a flight; superseded by the diagnosis at the epilepsy consultation.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-2">
    <td>Focal epilepsy with structural cause: focal to bilateral tonic-clonic seizure from a left orbitofrontal cavernous malformation, with left frontal epileptiform discharges on EEG; epilepsy under the ILAE 2014 practical definition (an unprovoked seizure with a recurrence risk above 60% from an epileptogenic lesion, and earlier seizures by history); synthetic modern detail linked to her publicly reported seizures and the coroner&apos;s finding</td><td>active</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>G40.209</td><td>S — Synthetic seizure type, EEG and classification; the seizures and the cavernous angioma of the left orbital frontal cerebrum are documented (D).</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-3">
    <td>Solitary cerebral cavernous malformation, 1.4 cm, left orbitofrontal cortex, with a hemosiderin rim and an adjacent developmental venous anomaly, no other lesion on susceptibility-weighted imaging (sporadic pattern); no acute hemorrhage</td><td>resolved</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>Q28.3</td><td>S — Synthetic imaging detail; the coroner reported a congenital cavernous angioma of the left orbital frontal cerebrum (D).</td>
  </tr></tbody></table></text>

